# Wireworm — Controls

Wireworm is played on the keyboard alone. This file defines every action the game
answers to and the keys bound to it. What the cursor does when it moves and fires
is in `specs/cursor.md`, and the screens the menu actions move between are in
`specs/ui.md`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
## How input reaches the game

This game stands on no engine, so the keyboard belongs to the runtime layer you
write. It listens to the page and reports which keys are down, by their
`KeyboardEvent.code` values, along with the press edge of each. Every key below
is named by its `code`, so a binding is a physical key rather than a
layout-dependent character.
{{else}}
## How input reaches the game

Input reaches the game as named actions from the {{engine.name}} engine, which
documents them in the `engine/` directory at the root of this repository. The
game registers the layout `LAYOUT` (`dpad-4-two-buttons`), which carries four-way
movement and two buttons, and registers every action in `ACTIONS` against the
keys `BINDINGS` gives it. `BINDINGS` names each key by its `KeyboardEvent.code`,
so a binding is a physical key rather than a layout-dependent character.

The whole of `ACTIONS` is registered, so both buttons of the layout fire.
{{/if}}

## The actions

| Action | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `up` | Moves the cursor up inside the band during play, and moves a menu highlight up. |
| `down` | Moves the cursor down inside the band during play, and moves a menu highlight down. |
| `left` | Moves the cursor left during play. |
| `right` | Moves the cursor right during play. |
| `a` | Fires a bolt. |
| `b` | Fires a bolt. |
| `confirm` | Accepts the highlighted menu item. |
| `back` | Leaves the current screen. |
| `pause` | Pauses live play, opening the pause screen. |
| `mute` | Toggles sound, from any screen. |

The four movement actions and the two fire actions are read as holds: the cursor
travels while a movement is held, and a held fire action produces a bolt every
`FIRE_INTERVAL` while the cooldown and the cap allow one, as `specs/cursor.md`
states. `confirm`, `back`, `pause`, and `mute` are read as press edges, once per
press.

## The bindings

| Action | Keys |
| --- | --- |
| `up` | `ArrowUp`, `KeyW` |
| `down` | `ArrowDown`, `KeyS` |
| `left` | `ArrowLeft`, `KeyA` |
| `right` | `ArrowRight`, `KeyD` |
| `a` | `Space` |
| `b` | `Space` |
| `confirm` | `Enter`, `Space` |
| `back` | `Escape` |
| `pause` | `KeyP`, `Escape` |
| `mute` | `KeyM` |

`Space` drives `a`, `b`, and `confirm`, and `Escape` drives `back` and `pause`.
The screen decides which one applies: `Space` fires while the game is being
played and confirms on a screen showing a menu; `Escape` pauses while the game is
being played and leaves the screen otherwise.

## Menus

Every menu is vertical and is driven by `up`, `down`, and `confirm`. `up` and
`down` move the highlight by one item and wrap at both ends, so moving down from
the last item highlights the first and moving up from the first highlights the
last. `confirm` takes the highlighted item. `specs/ui.md` states each screen's
items and where each one leads.

## The debug overlay

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The read-only debug overlay `specs/instrumentation.md` describes is shown and
hidden by the backtick key, `KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`. It is off when the
game starts. The overlay and its toggle are part of the runtime layer you write,
and they are not registered as an action.
{{else}}
The read-only debug overlay is the engine's, shown and hidden by the backtick
key, `KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`, and off when the game starts. It is not
one of Wireworm's actions. What it shows is in `specs/instrumentation.md`.
{{/if}}
